To create a Rust application that supports the Eleven Labs API and reads the contents of the clipboard aloud using Text-to-Speech (TTS), you can follow these steps:

1. Install Rust:
   Follow the instructions on the official Rust website to install Rust: https://www.rust-lang.org/tools/install

2. Create a new Rust project:
   Open a terminal and run the following command to create a new Rust project:

   ```
   cargo new elevenlabs_tts --bin
   ```

3. Add required dependencies:
   Navigate to the project directory:

   ```
   cd elevenlabs_tts
   ```

   Add the following dependencies to your `Cargo.toml` file:

   ```toml
   [dependencies]
   clipboard = "3.0.0"
   reqwest = { version = "0.11.10", features = ["json"] }
   serde = { version = "1.0.136", features = ["derive"] }
   serde_json = "1.0.81"
   ```

   These dependencies include clipboard for reading the clipboard, reqwest for making HTTP requests, serde for parsing JSON, and serde_json for serializing JSON.

4. Implement the main function:
   Open the `src/main.rs` file and replace its content with the following code:

   ```rust
   use clipboard::ClipboardProvider;
   use reqwest::blocking::get;
   use serde_json::json;
   use std::env;

   const API_KEY: &str = "YOUR_API_KEY";
   const API_URL: &str = "https://api.elevenlabs.io/v1/text-to-speech";

   async fn speak(text: &str) {
       let response = get(&format!("{}/synthesize", API_URL))
           .header("Authorization", format!("Bearer {}", API_KEY))
           .json(&json!({ "text": text }))
           .send()
           .await
           .expect("Failed to send request");

       let audio_data = response.bytes().await.expect("Failed to read response");
       // Save the audio data to a file and play it using a media player or a library like `rodio`
       // ...
   }

   fn main() {
       let clipboard = ClipboardProvider::new().unwrap();
       let text = clipboard.get_string().unwrap_or_default();

       if text.is_empty() {
           println!("No text found in clipboard.");
           return;
       }

       tokio::runtime::Builder::new_multi_thread()
           .build_async()
           .expect("Failed to create tokio runtime")
           .block_on(async {
               speak(&text).await;
           });
   }
   ```

   Replace `YOUR_API_KEY` with your Eleven Labs API key.

5. Build and run the application:
   Run the following command to build the application:

   ```
   cargo build --release
   ```

   After building, you can run the application with:

   ```
   ./target/release/elevenlabs_tts
   ```

   The application will read the text from the clipboard and use the Eleven Labs API to convert it to speech. However, it doesn't currently play the audio. You can save the audio data to a file and use a media player or a library like `rodio` to play it.

